 {"defaultPrevented":true,"key":"ArrowLeft","target":"#notes","type":"keydown"},
 {"defaultPrevented":false,"key":" ","target":"#notes","type":"keydown"}
}
So ArrowLeft keydown had defaultPrevented:true, meaning something prevented default (likely pikaday's _onKeyChange handler). Indeed pikaday adds keydown listener to document when keyboardInput:true (default true). In _onKeyChange, they handle left/right/up/down arrow keys to adjust date, and they call e.preventDefault().

Thus when the pikaday calendar is bound to the delivery-date field, but they have bound:false? Actually in index.html they set bound: false. However pikaday still adds document keydown listener if opts.keyboardInput is true (default true). They didn't set keyboardInput:false. So even though bound:false, they still listen globally for keydown and adjust date when arrow keys pressed, preventing default.

Thus ArrowLeft in notes input triggers pikaday's handler, preventing default, so caret doesn't move. Need to modify pikaday to only handle arrow keys when the pikaday widget is visible? Or when the focused element is the field bound to pikaday? Usually they check if the picker is visible (this.isVisible()) before handling keys. Look at _onKeyChange: they have:

if (self.isVisible()) {
    switch(e.keyCode){ ... }
}

But in observed keyEvents, the target is #notes input, not the field. Yet self.isVisible()? The calendar is visible? Probably yes because the calendar is displayed (container). The pikaday instance is bound to delivery-date field but bound:false, but they still show calendar? They attached container: document.getElementById('calendar'), and they didn't set bound:true, but they still call this.show() in constructor if !opts.bound? Let's see: In constructor, after setting up, they have:

if (opts.bound) {
    this.hide();
    self.el.className += ' is-bound';
    addEvent(opts.trigger, 'click', self._onInputClick);
    addEvent(opts.trigger, 'focus', self._onInputFocus);
    addEvent(opts.trigger, 'blur', self._onInputBlur);
} else {
    this.show();
}

Since bound:false, they go to else branch and call this.show(). So the picker is always visible (displayed in container). Therefore self.isVisible() returns true, so _onKeyChange processes arrow keys regardless of which element has focus, causing prevention.

We need to adjust so that arrow keys only affect date picker when the picker is visible AND the focused element is the bound field (or maybe the trigger). Since bound:false, trigger is field (opts.trigger = opts.field). But we could check if the activeElement is opts.field (or maybe opts.trigger). If not, let default behavior happen.

